#!/bin/sh
set -eu

# PUSHING IS CHEAP. THE GATE IS CI.
#
# This hook used to run the two containerized suites before letting a push
# leave the machine — the whole Go module under -race, the whole vitest suite.
# They are gone. What is left is the issue tracker, which is not a test and
# costs a second.
#
# Measured here on 2026-08-28 (12 cores, 16 GiB, the OrbStack VM capped at
# 7.8 GiB), pushing a branch whose entire diff was frontend/ and
# .beads/issues.jsonl:
#
#   go test -race (container)    273 %CPU   1.99 GiB
#   vitest        (container)    455 %CPU   1.72 GiB
#   another worktree's vitest    369 %CPU   2.10 GiB
#   load average                 4.3 -> 21.6 over four minutes, still running
#
# Two of those three were this hook, and the Go one had no business running at
# all: there is no .go file in that diff. The scoping could not see it. git
# hands a pre-push hook the REMOTE's sha for every ref, and for a branch the
# remote has never seen that sha is forty zeros — no range, nothing to diff, so
# the hook took its conservative branch and ran everything. Every task in this
# repo gets its own worktree and its own branch, so the first push of a branch
# IS the push, and "scoped to what the push actually changes" described a code
# path that almost never executed.
#
# That was fixable — the commits being pushed are `git rev-list <tip> --not
# --remotes=<remote>` whether or not the remote knows the branch. It was not
# fixed, because fixing it would only have made a heavy gate fire half as
# often, and a push has to be cheap for a reason that has nothing to do with
# how well it is scoped: a gate a person waits four minutes for is a gate they
# learn to pass with --no-verify, and then it guards nothing at all.
#
# WHAT CATCHES A BREAK INSTEAD, in the order it bites:
#
#   - pre-commit, untouched. Static, seconds: formatting, linting, the
#     ratchets, the wire contracts, the type checkers. That is what keeps a
#     commit both cheap and honest.
#   - CI, which is the source of truth and always was. It runs the four jobs in
#     the environments they belong to, and a developer Mac is not one of them —
#     AGENTS.md records internal/pty hanging to its 600s panic here while green
#     on the runner.
#   - `make ci-full` on the MERGED tree, by whoever integrates, before main.
#
# And the suites are still one command away when you want them by hand — the
# runners stayed, and three test failure messages already point at them:
#
#   sh -c '. ./.githooks/containerized-tests.sh; go_test_containerized'
#   sh -c '. ./.githooks/containerized-tests.sh; vitest_containerized'

# Some developers install bd with `go install`, which puts it in GOPATH/bin
# rather than on the default PATH. beads-hook.sh returns 0 when bd is missing —
# a deliberate skip for someone contributing without beads — so without this
# line their pushes would silently stop syncing the tracker, which is the exact
# failure the block below exists to prevent.
command -v go >/dev/null 2>&1 && PATH="$(go env GOPATH)/bin:$PATH"

# Push the issue tracker along with the code.
#
# The issue database goes to refs/dolt/data on the remote, and that is what a
# fresh clone reads — there is no tracked .beads/issues.jsonl in this repository
# to fall back to. So a push that skips this step leaves collaborators on a
# backlog that can be days old, with nothing on screen to suggest it.

. "$(dirname "$0")/beads-hook.sh"

# The spare copy first, from the local database, to a ref of its own.
#
# Before push_beads_state and not after, because this script runs under `set -e`
# and push_beads_state deliberately returns nonzero when bd dolt push genuinely
# fails. That failure IS the scenario the snapshot exists for — a stranded
# refs/dolt/data with the healthy backlog only on this machine. Publishing
# afterwards would mean never publishing in the one case that matters.
publish_beads_snapshot

push_beads_state
